---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { QUALITIES, CONTINUA, PROFILES, RUBRIC_VERSION, continuumLabel } from '../lib/rubric';
import { faqJsonLd, breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How are places scored on Work in Ozaukee?',
    a: 'Seven dimensions. Five are quality scales from 1 to 5 — wifi, power, seat, welcome and calls — where one end is simply worse. Two are continua between opposite but equally valid poles: sound runs hushed to lively, pace runs lingering to brisk. Every level has a written description, so scoring means picking the sentence that matches rather than choosing a number by feel.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is there no single star rating?',
    a: 'Because the honest answer to "is this place good?" is "for what?" A wine bar that is hopeless for a video call can be the best room in town for three hours of writing. Instead of one average, the same scores are read four ways — deep work, calls, a quick session, and company — each of which wants something different.',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How places are scored — Work in Ozaukee"
  description="The rubric behind every score: five quality scales, two preference continua, and four ways of reading them depending on what kind of day you're having."
  jsonLd={[
    faqJsonLd(faqs),
    breadcrumbJsonLd([
      { name: 'Where to go, today', path: '/' },
      { name: 'How scoring works', path: '/scoring' },
    ]),
  ]}
>
  <h1>How scoring works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every place is judged the same way, and this page is here so you can check my work —
    if you think a score is wrong, you can point at the exact sentence you'd have picked
    instead.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A place gets scored once, not every visit. Outlets, seating and whether anyone minds
      you taking a call are properties of the room, not of a Tuesday. When somewhere
      changes, or I've been enough times to think I got it wrong, I re-score it and the
      date updates.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Five things that are simply better or worse</h2>
  <p class="note">
    Scored 1 to 5. Anchors written at 1, 3 and 5 — I use 2 and 4 for "between these two."
  </p>

  {
    QUALITIES.map((d) => (
      <>
        <h3>{d.label} — {d.question}</h3>
        <ul class="stack">
          <li><b>1</b> — {d.anchors[1]}</li>
          <li><b>3</b> — {d.anchors[3]}</li>
          <li><b>5</b> — {d.anchors[5]}</li>
        </ul>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Two things that are just tradeoffs</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      These aren't quality scales, because neither end is wrong. A hushed room and a
      lively one are both somebody's ideal — often the same person on different days.
      Scoring them 1 to 5 would imply one end is a failure, so instead they run between
      two named poles, and it's up to you which end you're looking for.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    CONTINUA.map((d) => (
      <>
        <h3>{d.label} — {d.question}</h3>
        <p class="meta">{d.low} ←→ {d.high}</p>
        <ul class="stack">
          <li><b>{d.low}</b> — {d.anchors['-2']}</li>
          <li><b>In between</b> — {d.anchors['0']}</li>
          <li><b>{d.high}</b> — {d.anchors['2']}</li>
        </ul>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Four ways of reading the same scores</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The honest answer to "is this place good?" is "for what?" So rather than average
      everything into one number that hides the tradeoff, the same seven scores get read
      four ways. On the continua each profile has a <em>target</em> rather than a
      preference for "high" — deep work wants hushed, company wants lively, and the same
      score reads as a match or a mismatch depending on which you asked for.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ul class="cards">
    {
      PROFILES.map((p) => (
        <li class="card">
          <h3>{p.label}</h3>
          <p>{p.blurb}</p>
          <ul class="attrs">
            {Object.entries(p.qualities).map(([dim, w]) => (
              <li>{dim} <b>{Math.round((w as number) * 100)}%</b></li>
            ))}
            {Object.entries(p.targets).map(([dim, spec]) => {
              const def = CONTINUA.find((c) => c.key === dim)!;
              const s = spec as { target: number; weight: number };
              return (
                <li>
                  wants <b>{continuumLabel(def, s.target)}</b> {def.label.toLowerCase()}{' '}
                  <b>{Math.round(s.weight * 100)}%</b>
                </li>
              );
            })}
          </ul>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      If I haven't scored enough of what a profile needs, it says so instead of showing a
      number. A confident-looking "4.6" built from one answer is worse than an admission
      that I didn't check.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Rubric version {RUBRIC_VERSION}. Every scored place records which version it was
      judged under, so rewriting these anchors later won't quietly mix old scores in with
      new ones.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
